Yeah, looking forward to working with you guys.

20
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Hello everyone, I'm Herman. A year ago, I didn't really know how LLMs worked.

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
So I think for me, tokens were the things you collect in video games and attention was the thing you had like the attention economy.

22
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Then after spending a lot of time on this course last year, I'm now doing LLM research and I'm really excited to TA this year.

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Hi everyone, I'm Steven. I am a first time CA for this course and I'm very excited about it.

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
I think it'll be a lot of fun. Broadly in my research, I work on language models, theory, and some data efficiency stuff.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
And I'm excited to meet you all.

26
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Alright, so let's go into things.

27
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
So this is the third time we're offering it.

28
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Last year we decided to put all our lectures on YouTube, so some of you have seen it.

29
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So what's new?

30
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Well, what hasn't changed is the from scratch philosophy.

31
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,000
We still believe strongly that by building everything from the ground up, you really learn how everything works.

32
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Of course, we don't actually build everything up from scratch because that wouldn't fit in a quarter.

33
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
So over the last two years we've been refining our recipe and figuring out what are the things that you build up from scratch that are most high value.

34
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
And then finally, as Tatsu alluded to, even in a year, a lot has changed.

35
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
I think this year we're going to spend maybe a bit more time on mixture of experts.

36
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:22,000
And of course, agents are very popular these days, so getting a handle on long context and what is needed for that are going to be important.

37
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
So why do we make this course?

38
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,000
And I think the problem two years ago was that researchers were becoming disconnected from the underlying technology.

39
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:43,000
It used to be the case maybe ten years ago, all AI researchers would just implement and train their own models.

40
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
And then even eight years ago, people would download pre-trained models such as BERT and fine tune them.

41
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And I think a lot of today, you can get by by simply prompting a model.

42
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And of course there's nothing wrong with prompting models.

43
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
You know, I think you can do amazing things with it.

44
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
I think moving up the abstraction in general is a great thing, but abstractions are leaky.

45
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And sometimes, I'm sure all of you have prompted models, you run into situations where something is,

46
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
you wanted to do something but you just can't do it and there's no recourse.

47
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And I would argue that if you're really interested in fundamental research,

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
by simply prompting a model, you're sort of vastly constraining the set of options,

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
the design space you're looking at.

50
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
And by looking at fundamental research, you really need to tear up the whole stack.

51
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
So, I would argue that full understanding of how language models work is really necessary for

52
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
fundamental research.

53
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
And the way that we're going to get our understanding is by building.

54
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
That's the philosophy of this class.

55
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
But there's one small problem, which is that industrialization of language models has happened.

56
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Frontier models are really, really expensive.

57
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Even, this is three years ago, GPT-4 was supposedly costing $100 million to train.

58
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And now, costs probably are on the order of a billion, although that's speculative.

59
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
And the number of GPUs that all the big labs are building is just kind of immense.

60
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Furthermore, there's no details on how many of these models are built.

61
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
So, even back in 2023, the GPT-4 paper explicitly says that due to competitive landscape and

62
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
safety implications, we're not going to share anything about how the models are being built.

63
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
So, these frontier models are in some sense out of the reach for us.

64
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Now, we could build small language models, and we will build small language models.

65
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:49,000
But, this, I think it's important to remember that these might not be representative of the actual frontier models.

66
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And I'll give you two examples why this might be the case.

67
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
So, here's one post.

68
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
This is from actually quite a long time ago.

69
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
I think back in 2021.

70
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
We're going to spend more time on flop counting and looking at where the compute is being spent.

71
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:14,000
But if you look at small scales, the fraction of flops spent in the MLP layers is about 44%.

72
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
And if you scale up to 175B, then it goes to 80%.

73
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
So, what you optimize and what matters at large scale is going to be different from small scale.

74
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So, if you did a bunch of small scale stuff here on the attention, you might not experience the same benefits at large scale.

75
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:41,000
The second example is that we know of emergence of behavior with scale.

76
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:51,000
So, small models, this is again from a while ago, but even back then, if you have zero shot or few shot learning of various tasks,

77
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
it basically seemed like nothing was working.

78
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And only when you reach a critical scale, do you suddenly see a lot of improvement.

79
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:08,000
So, again, if you're working on small scale, you might not see certain types of phenomena compared to if you were working at full scale.

80
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Okay, so that might be a little bit disheartening.

81
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
But fear not, we're going to learn something in this class.

82
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And the question is, what can we learn that actually transfers?

83
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Okay, and I think it's important to break this down into three types of knowledge.

84
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
First, there's the mechanics of how things work, what a Transformer is,

85
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
how model parallelism works, right?

86
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
There's mindset, which is how do you go about approaching building a language model.

87
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
We're going to talk about how you want to squeeze the most out of your hardware,

88
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:42,000
taking scaling seriously.

89
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:48,000
And finally, intuitions, which data modeling decisions are going to yield good performance.

90
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:54,000
So now, in this class, I think we can do a pretty good job of teaching the mechanics,

91
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
which is how things work, and the mindset.

92
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:03,000
And we're going to really emphasize that you profile and benchmark everything and try to optimize for efficiency.

93
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
These things do transfer to a larger scale.

94
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Now, the intuitions about what modeling decisions and what data decisions do work might not necessarily transfer across scales.

95
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,000
For that, you actually have to go somewhere where you can do things at scale.

96
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:33,000
So, on the note of intuitions, it is worth remarking that some design decisions are just not justifiable,

97
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and just purely come from experimentation.

98
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Whereas mechanics, you can kind of, by construction, see how the parallelism and

99
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
how kernels are going to speed things up and so on.

100
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:49,000
But for intuitions about what modeling changes work, I think you just have to run experiments.

101
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
There's this famous Noam Chomsky paper that introduces the SwiGLU activation,

102
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
which we're going to look about.

103
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
And in the conclusion section, he very honestly has this final sentence, which says,

104
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
we offer no explanation.

105
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
We attribute their success of these architectures, all else, to divine benevolence.

106
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
So that in some sense is something that you just have to gain from experience.

107
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Final note about the bitter lesson, which I think has been circulating and people talk about it.

108
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
I think there's a sort of a common misconception here, what it means.

109
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
I think the wrong interpretation is that scale is all that matters.

110
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Algorithms don't matter. But that's not correct.

111
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
The right interpretation is that algorithms that scale are what matters.

112
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:48,000
And you can think about very simply that the accuracy of your model is basically your efficiency times the resources.

113
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
So efficiency is output over input and resources is the input.

114
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:58,000
And efficiency is actually in some sense way more important at larger scale.

115
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Right?

116
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:08,000
If you're doing a small scale experiment, if your run takes twice as long, maybe you just wait twice as long and then you come back later.

117
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:13,000
But if you're doing things at scale, you know, that could be hundreds of millions of dollars.

118
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And you definitely don't want to do that.

119
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
Even like a 5% improvement might be a big deal.

120
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
So in fact, efficiency is actually really, really critical.

121
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And I hope to bake that into your kind of mindset as one of the consequences of this class.

122
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And empirically, if you look at this paper from OpenAI in 2020 that showed there's a 44x algorithmic efficiency on ImageNet between 2012 and 2019.

123
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And it's surely the case that hardware did get a lot better.

124
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
But that also comes with algorithmic improvements.

125
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And of course, when you multiply them together, that's when you see like a huge bump in efficiency and accuracy.

126
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:09,000
So the framing with all that said is, what is the best model one can build with a certain data and compute budget?

127
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:17,000
For pre-training, it's mostly we're going to talk about compute budget because we're going to try and assume that we have a lot more data than we have compute.

128
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:29,000
But if you're in a setting where you're data limited or you have stashed away actually tons of B200s, then you might be data bound.

129
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
So in other words, maximize efficiency.

130
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
And we're going to see this theme come up throughout the class.

131
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Okay.

132
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:46,000
So next, I want to spend a bit of time talking about language models and a bit of a history and just contextualization before we jump into the,

133
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
more technical details.

134
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
So language models have been around for a while.

135
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:57,000
Shannon back in the 50s was using language models to measure the entropy of English.

136
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:05,000
And for a long time, n-gram models were used actually in machine translation and speech recognition systems.

137
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:12,000
And they weren't the whole system, but they were an important part of making sure that you generate a fluid text.

138
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:20,000
I would say that the lineage of the modern language models comes from neural architectures.

139
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:27,000
And so there is a bunch of ideas, I think, which are important to this development.

140
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So in the 90s, there was LSTMs.

141
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Joshua Bengio actually wrote the first neural language model in paper back in 2003.

142
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,000
This was actually not LSTM.

143
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
This was just a feedforward network that looked at the small context.

144
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And then there was a seq2seq modeling, which boldly said we can actually compress a whole sentence into a vector.

145
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,000
The Atom optimizer attention mechanism, which was developed for machine translation.

146
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
The transformer architecture, which built on top of that, which was also developed for machine translation.

147
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:08,000
And then scaling up to a mixture of experts, model parallelism.

148
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:14,000
You see a lot of different architecture and also systems and optimizer ideas.

149
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And developed in the 2010s.

150
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:23,000
And then by the late 2010s, I think things were starting to get really interesting.

151
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
So there was the ELMo and BERT.

152
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,000
These are language models that were trained on lots of text.

153
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,000
And then you could fine tune them on some downstream tasks like question answering.

154
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
And it would show a huge improvement on that.

155
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:43,000
So the model there was, you know, take one of these models and then fine tune.

156
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:51,000
And then Google had a paper that was really, I think, foreshadowing this view of prompt and response out.

157
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So it was really, I think, OpenAI that really opened up the floodgates here by embracing scaling.

158
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,000
So they had a GPT paper back in 2018 or so.

159
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
They scaled up to GPT-2.

160
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:12,000
And then they really figured out, you know, or embraced the idea of scaling laws,

161
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:19,000
which we're going to talk about in a second, which enabled them to train GPT-3,

162
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:26,000
which was a much more massive, almost more than 10x the larger model at the time.

163
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
And it could show emergent behavior like in-context learning.

164
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:36,000
At that point, you know, Google was saying, okay, we need to do something too.

165
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
So they trained a massive model.

166
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
It turned out to be kind of under-trained.

167
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
And it turned out that their DeepMind, which was not integrated with Google at the time,

168
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
had figured out, you know, optimal, compute optimal scaling laws.

169
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
So all this was happening.

170
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:58,000
And after GPT-3 came out, I think for many folks, this was sort of kind of a wake-up call.

171
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,000
And at that time, there were a lot of early attempts to, let's try to replicate this.

172
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:08,000
So there was a grassroots organization called Eleuther that created some open data sets and models.

173
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
They weren't very large because they didn't have much compute.

174
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:20,000
Meta's first LLM, you could see, tell that it was a replication because it was like 175 billion parameters.

175
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
But it was not a very good model.

176
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
They ran into a lot of hardware issues.

177
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:31,000
And then there was another Hugging Face, a big science project.

178
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,000
So these models were, I would say, not very strong.

179
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Then in the last three years, I think the open model ecosystem has changed quite a bit.

180
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:46,000
With Meta kind of leading the way with a LLM series of models, LLM, LLM2, LLM3.

181
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Mistral got in the game.

182
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
And then a whole set of Chinese models.

183
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I think I'm missing some, like ByteDance has something.

184
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
And I think Tencent probably has some other stuff.

185
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
So it's hard to keep track of everything.

186
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
But everyone's heard of DeepSeek and Qwen.

187
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
So I think I got the main ones.

188
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:14,000
But I think what's interesting and exciting about these is now we have open weight models that are approaching closed models.

189
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
So depending on who you ask and how you benchmark, they might be a little bit behind or comparable.

190
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:25,000
But there are definitely very, very credible models that are being widely used in industry.

191
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:32,000
Now there's another line of work which is going beyond just releasing open weight models.

192
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:37,000
AI2, NVIDIA, and the Marin project which I work on.

193
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:51,000
We try to provide not just the weights but the paper and the code and the data so that we can understand how these models are built in a more thorough way.

194
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Why do I emphasize this open ecosystem so much?

195
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Mostly because this course would not be possible I think without these models.

196
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:10,000
By the fact that there are still many papers that are being published about how these big old MOEs and RL systems are working.

197
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Enables us to at least glimpse into how these frontier models are being built and trying to triangulate the pieces.

198
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:22,000
Now I think of course a lot of the details even with these like Qwen papers.

199
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
I think they're missing a lot of details so you can't reproduce them.

200
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Notably the data mixture is something that we don't know.

201
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
But I think it's better, much better than nothing.

202
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Okay, so in the last decade I think the idea of what a language model is has changed, right?

203
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
It used to be something that you fine-tuned.

204
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And then it was something you prompt.

205
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:50,000
And now in the ChatGPT era it was something you talk to and that you can have a, you know,

206
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
conversation with.

207
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:58,000
And now, okay, I guess I don't have internet. That's fine.

208
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
And now we're in the era of agents.

209
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
If you click on this link it basically shows you a giant agent trace.

210
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:11,000
And I'm still kind of, it's mind boggling how strong some of these models are.

211
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:16,000
You give it like a page of text and it does some really complicated agentic coding task.

212
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:25,000
So, you know, what we demand of our language models today is probably beyond the imagination of anyone back, you know, ten years ago.

213
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,000
That said, I think the fundamentals, I think, haven't changed that much.

214
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Largely we still build on GPUs and kernels.

215
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:38,000
We still optimize using gradient or stochastic gradient like approaches.

216
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:43,000
We still have the transformer in attention and, you know, we'll talk a little bit more about architectures.

217
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,000
But it hasn't changed that much.

218
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
I think the specs are different.

219
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Now we demand greater context lengths, which means that inference efficiency matters even more.

220
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,000
So the good news for us is that we didn't have to completely change this class.

221
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Only Tatsu section on the latest, you know, Chinese architectures.

