313
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,000
So let's talk about what we're going to cover in this class.

314
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:30,000
So there's basically five parts mirroring the five assignments that you'll have.

315
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:33,000
Basic systems, scaling laws, data, and alignment.

316
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:41,000
So I'm going to now go through each part and just give you a taste of what you will learn.

317
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:42,000
Okay.

318
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:48,000
So in the basics, this is basically the first two weeks.

319
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,000
The goal is to just be able to train a language model and build it from scratch.

320
00:27:52,000 --> 00:28:00,000
So the components here are, we're going to tokenize the data, we're going to define architecture, and then we're going to implement optimizer and train it.

321
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:01,000
Okay?

322
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,000
So then you wonder, like, what are the rest of the classes for?

323
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:08,000
Well, we'll get to there. So let's start with tokenization.

324
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:14,000
Tokenization is really about what are the atoms that the model operates on.

325
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:27,000
And, you know, formally a tokenizer converts between raw inputs, which are just bytes, and a sequence of integers, which represent the tokens.

326
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
Conceptually, it's a segmentation of the text.

327
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:41,000
We're going to talk about the byte-pair encoding, BPE tokenizer, which intuitively breaks the input into frequently occurring chunks.

328
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:45,000
And then, remember, this class is about maximizing efficiency.

329
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:55,000
So through an efficiency lens, tokenization is good because it takes a long sequence, if you just think about the raw byte stream, and reduces it into a smaller number of tokens.

330
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:01,000
But more subtly, but maybe more importantly, it allows you to do adaptive computation.

331
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:14,000
So some, maybe some places are actually a lot of bytes, but actually it should be compressed into one token, whereas some of the more rare or interesting parts of input should be left as multiple tokens.

332
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:17,000
Okay, we'll talk more about this.

333
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:23,000
I just want to mention that, you know, every year I'm hoping that I don't have to teach tokenization.

334
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:29,000
Because the dream is to really have an end-to-end way that directly operates on bytes.

335
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:39,000
And there's been a number of works, including, you know, recently, there's been this H-Net work that seems promising.

336
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:45,000
But so far these have not been scaled to the frontier, and since the frontier models are still using tokenizers,

337
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:50,000
we felt like it would still be wise to teach tokenizers.

338
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:56,000
Okay, so now, after you tokenize your input, you have a bunch of tokens.

339
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
Now you define a model on top.

340
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:02,000
And everyone, I think, has a familiarity with the original, the Transformer.

341
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:09,000
And if you take CS224N, the NLP class, then you've seen Transformers.

342
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:16,000
Since then, I think there have been a lot of improvements or refinements to Transformers,

343
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
which I think are important.

344
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
And Tatsu is going to talk more about this in a bit.

345
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:26,000
But just to run through a set of types of things that one might have to think about.

346
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:31,000
So, the activation functions have evolved.

347
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:34,000
How do you positional encodings—has evolved.

348
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:42,000
How you normalize the different layers to prevent blowup has evolved.

349
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:49,000
Instead of doing full attention, there's many ways to basically reduce the attention computation

350
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:55,000
because the attention is N squared and it's, where N is a sequence length, and that gets really expensive.

351
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:58,000
So there's a bunch of ideas around that.

352
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:06,000
If you're more ambitious, you can look at these state-space models or, you know, equivalently linear attention,

353
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,000
like Mamba and gated delta net.

354
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:12,000
These have been popular in the last, you know, few years.

355
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:17,000
And usually some hybrid between these models and attention seems to work, you know, quite well.

356
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:20,000
So we'll be exploring some of that.

357
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:27,000
And then within the MLP layers of the Transformer, the original Transformer was just a dense MLP.

358
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:35,000
And now, mixture of experts has become the sort of dominant paradigm for building compute-efficient Transformers.

359
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:37,000
So we're going to talk about that.

360
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:42,000
And of course, with MLP, mixture of experts, you know, it's not just defining architecture,

361
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:48,000
but we'll see that we'll also need different techniques for training the model.

362
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:53,000
And then finally, perhaps somewhat boringly, but an important question is, you know,

363
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:56,000
what is the shape of your transformer? How many layers?

364
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:00,000
How many heads? What is hidden dimension, number of experts?

365
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:08,000
This might come in more as we talk about scaling laws, but setting these is actually, seems kind of, you know, almost trivial.

366
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:16,000
It's a hyperparameter, but actually in the context of scaling of language models has a huge, huge implication.

367
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:17,000
Okay.

368
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:22,000
So, once you define your model architecture, how do you train the model?

369
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:27,000
And here, there's a bunch of design decisions around the loss function.

370
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:31,000
There's next word, next token prediction, which is the default.

371
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:40,000
But people have found that looking, predicting more than one token seems to be helpful for improving the model.

372
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:44,000
And there's optimizers.

373
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:50,000
People used to use Adam, but increasingly, Muon has been used, especially with some of the latest open

374
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:56,000
models, such as the Kimi K2 models.

375
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:08,000
Initialization, which is, again, sounds kind of boring, but turns out to have a huge impact on how your ability and the training stability of larger models.

376
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:17,000
Learning rates, schedule, regularization, batch size, and then MOE specific things.

377
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:21,000
So, you know, you look at this list and you might think, well, these are just hyperparameters.

378
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:24,000
I'm going to try a bunch of different options out.

379
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:39,000
But it turns out that, you know, really being very careful about setting these hyperparameters in a principled way will make the difference between a run that just blows up and is useless between a run that is achieving state of the art.

380
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:45,000
Okay, I'll come back to that point when we talk about scaling loss.

381
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:55,000
Okay, so then in assignment one, what you're going to do is you're going to implement the BPE tokenizer, implement the transformer, the loss function, optimizer, the whole training staff.

382
00:33:55,000 --> 00:34:01,000
We're going to make you do a bunch of resource accounting so you understand where your flops are going.

383
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:06,000
You're going to train some models on these data sets like tiny stories and open web text.

384
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:15,000
And then there's going to be a leaderboard that where you're going to try to drive down perplexity as fast as you can.

385
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:22,000
So, for those of you familiar with the nano GPT speedruns, it's kind of similar to that.

386
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:32,000
Okay, so by the end of assignment one, you should be able to walk away and build a language model from scratch.

387
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:34,000
So that's very exciting.

388
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:41,000
The, you know, if you have a, if I have a high level takeaway here, it's that, you know,

389
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:50,000
while the tokenizer and modeling and training are sort of presented as distinct pieces, it is actually, you know,

390
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:53,000
everything is about kind of balancing the following.

391
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:58,000
So you want expressive models because you want to represent the complexities of the data.

392
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:04,000
But at the same time, you want to, you're trained to be stable.

393
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:11,000
And we're going to see, talk a lot about how do you keep the parameter and gradient norms in this sort of

394
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
Goldilocks zone so they don't blow up and don't vanish.

395
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:19,000
It turns out a lot of training language models is about just, you know, stability.

396
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:25,000
And then finally efficiency, which is somewhat more straightforward.

397
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:27,000
You just make it run fast on hardware.

398
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:32,000
But you're going to see interesting things like, you know, if we change the architecture,

399
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:37,000
a lot of the architecture decisions are, well, we can make it faster by, let's say,

400
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:41,000
reducing the, the projecting of a lower dimensional space.

401
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,000
But then the question is, does it work as well?

402
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:51,000
And so making those trade-offs is something that is going to be sort of the name of the game here.

403
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,000
Okay.

404
00:35:53,000 --> 00:36:01,000
So, in assignment two, we're going to dive more deeply into, you know, systems.

405
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:05,000
And the goal here is just to get the most out of your hardware.

406
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:09,000
So we're going to talk about kernels, how you parallelize across multiple GPUs,

407
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,000
and how do you do inference.

408
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:17,000
So, the basics which we're actually going to start on next lecture,

409
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:19,000
I mentioned resource accounting.

410
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:26,000
And I think, you know, you've all probably built models before.

411
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:33,000
But this is really about keeping track of all, where all the flops go.

412
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:35,000
And where all the memory is being spent.

413
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:39,000
So we're going to spend some time basically doing the resource accounting.

414
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:43,000
We're going to see, you know, this formula that comes up.

415
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:47,000
How do you, how many flops is training a model on one trillion tokens?

416
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:50,000
Well, it's six times N times D roughly.

417
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:52,000
And where does that come from?

418
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:57,000
And then we're going to look at the hardware.

419
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:03,000
And here's a very cartoon picture of what to remark about hardware.

420
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:06,000
Is that your memory is not where your compute is.

421
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:11,000
And you have to move either your parameters or activations into,

422
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:14,000
from the memory to compute, do the compute, and move it back.

423
00:37:14,000 --> 00:37:18,000
Right? And that often is the bottleneck.

424
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:24,000
So, you know, for example, B200s, which we'll have the opportunity to play with.

425
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:33,000
It has 2.25 petaflops per second, BF16, and it has 8 terabytes a second of memory.

426
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:35,000
So, you know, what does that mean?

427
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:38,000
I think when we, I think I'll do this next lecture.

428
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:49,000
We're going to break this down and use this information to do some calculations and see how long different types of algorithms will need to take.

429
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:57,000
We're going to talk about roofline analysis, which allows us to understand whether a computation is bottlenecked by either compute or memory.

430
00:37:57,000 --> 00:38:01,000
In general, it is, you know, memory.

431
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:06,000
And then, talk a little bit about benchmarking and profiling.

432
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:13,000
Okay. So, here's what, you know, DGX H200 looks like.

433
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:19,000
You have eight GPUs, they're connected via, you know, NVLink.

434
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:28,000
And then, if you have many, if you have a thousand GPUs, then you would have multiple of these, and they're, you know, connected either in InfiniBand or Ethernet.

435
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:32,000
So, the next two parts when we talk about systems is, you know, kernels.

436
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:35,000
So, a kernel is basically a function that runs on the GPU.

437
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:47,000
And when you're just using plain PyTorch, you know, all the PyTorch primitives actually correspond to launching particular kernels, which are built in.

438
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:54,000
So, you're already using kernels, whether you know it or not.

439
00:38:54,000 --> 00:39:06,000
But the point is that, if, for certain types of computation, if you look at it, you can actually write custom kernels to make the GPUs go faster.

440
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:12,000
And the main principle here is organizing the compute to minimize data movement.

441
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:22,000
So, remember this picture, moving data from, from memory is expensive, so you want to try to minimize that.

442
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:27,000
So, just as a kind of a simple example, suppose you wanted to compute A and B.

443
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:36,000
So, often you would have to read from your high bandwidth memory, HBM, compute it, write it back, and then read again, compute it, write it back.

444
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:40,000
Right? So, then you're basically sending the data back and forth twice.

445
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:47,000
And there's this idea called fusion where you read it once, do both of the computations, and you write it back.

446
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:52,000
And that will save you a lot of time.

447
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:53,000
So, that's operator fusion.

448
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:59,000
Tiling is kind of a more sophisticated variant around the same idea.

449
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:03,000
There's also, GPUs have also gotten a lot more complicated.

450
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:08,000
And I'm not sure how many of these details we'll have time to get into, but at least we want to expose you to some of the,

451
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:20,000
sort of the peculiarities, I would say, of GPUs and give you appreciation for the types of things that one has to consider in order to squeeze as much juice out of them as possible.

452
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:25,000
And we'll write some kernels in and try it.

453
00:40:25,000 --> 00:40:29,000
So, what happens if you have thousands of GPUs?

454
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:36,000
So, the principle of minimize data movement is still the same.

455
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:44,000
The only thing is that moving data between different GPUs is even more expensive.

456
00:40:44,000 --> 00:40:54,000
We're going to talk about how these very classic collective operations like gather and reduce and all reduce are the way to kind of think about,

457
00:40:54,000 --> 00:40:57,000
basically, distributed training.

458
00:40:57,000 --> 00:41:04,000
The general game is that we have these model parameters, we have activations, gradients, and optimizer states,

459
00:41:04,000 --> 00:41:08,000
and they need to be sharded or split across multiple GPUs.

460
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:13,000
And, of course, you need to bring the right data to the right nodes to make the compute and write it back.

461
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:23,000
So, there's a whole kind of orchestration that, and how to do that efficiently is going to be the topic of this unit.

462
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:26,000
And there's multiple ways to shard.

463
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:31,000
You can shard by splitting up your data, splitting up your model, splitting up different layers in the model,

464
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:37,000
splitting up the sequences, splitting up between experts, and we'll talk about the trade-offs that come with each of these.

465
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:46,000
Okay, and then finally, we're going to talk about inference, which, as I mentioned, is growing in importance.

466
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:49,000
So, the goal of inference is to actually use the model.

467
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:51,000
So, minor detail here.

468
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:56,000
So, inference is, of course, you need to use inference when you're chatting with the model,

469
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:00,000
but it also is useful for reinforcement learning.

470
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:07,000
It's useful for doing the rollouts, test time compute, generating synthetic data, evaluation.

471
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:15,000
So, inference is a very critical part of what it means to do language modeling work.

472
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:25,000
So, we're probably not going to spend as much time as I like on this because the course is already kind of filled up, but we'll see what we can do.

473
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:32,000
There was some discussion around whether we should make you write inference from scratch, but we'll see.

474
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:40,000
So, the way to think about inference is that there's two phases, a pre-fill and a decode.

475
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:46,000
In the pre-fill, you take the prompt, and then you feed all the tokens forward and build key-value pairs.

476
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:49,000
This is very much like what happens in training.

477
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:55,000
And then, in the decoding part, tokens are generated one at a time.

478
00:42:55,000 --> 00:43:04,000
And this is the part that becomes quickly memory bound, and this is why inference is hard.

479
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:08,000
And so, there's many things you can do to speed up inference.

480
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:12,000
You can try to use a cheaper model by pruning a larger model.

481
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:16,000
You can quantize, you can distill, you can use this technique called speculative decoding,

482
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:21,000
where you use a cheaper model to sort of run ahead and guess a bunch of tokens.

483
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:28,000
And now, you use the full model, which can operate on those tokens in parallel to see if it's good.

484
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:34,000
And if you got lucky, then you can accept all those tokens, and you're much faster than if you're doing one token at a time.

485
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:37,000
And of course, you can do systems optimizations.

486
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:42,000
There's a bunch of kernels that are designed specifically for inference.

487
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:46,000
And then, one of the interesting things about inference is that if you're running a service,

488
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:51,000
queries are coming at you at potentially different times.

489
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:54,000
And then, you have to figure out how to batch them up.

490
00:43:54,000 --> 00:44:00,000
Whereas, in training, you basically are already defined your batches, and everything is much more predictable.

491
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:14,000
So, in assignment two, there's going to be implementing kernels in Triton, and doing some sort of parallel training.

492
00:44:14,000 --> 00:44:23,000
The details here might actually change as the CAs have grand plans of revamping the systems.

493
00:44:23,000 --> 00:44:32,000
So, the assignment might look a bit different from last year's, but it will cover roughly the same material.

494
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:39,000
One thing I will mention is that there's this wonderful book out of some Google people called How to Scale Your Model.

495
00:44:39,000 --> 00:44:50,000
And I think it's really nice for providing a conceptual understanding of roofline analysis and transformer math and doing LMS conceptually.

496
00:44:50,000 --> 00:44:54,000
So, I highly recommend you take a look at that.

497
00:44:54,000 --> 00:44:59,000
Now, the only thing is that it is from Google, so it's about TPUs.

498
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:03,000
But a lot of the high-level concepts are similar.

499
00:45:03,000 --> 00:45:06,000
And now, they have a new chapter on how to think about GPUs.

500
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:16,000
Okay. So, the third assignment is about scaling laws.

501
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:20,000
So, by now, you've trained a language model.

502
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:26,000
You can make it go really fast by optimizing kernels in parallel.

503
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:30,000
Now, you want to scale up. So, how do you scale up?

504
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:32,000
So, imagine the following setting.

505
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:41,000
If you had 1e25 flops, so this is tens of millions of dollars of compute, what model would you train?

506
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:42,000
Okay.

507
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:49,000
So, this is, I think, kind of a daunting task because if you mess up, well, that's a lot of money down the drain.

508
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:58,000
So, and you can't do your probably typical hyperparameter tuning at that scale because you only get to train one model.

509
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:11,000
And so, this is like the key problem that you have to deal with in language model, large language model training that you don't have to really deal with if you're just fine tuning a model or doing small scale stuff.

510
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:20,000
And so, the key conceptual shift here is that we shouldn't think about a single model that we're training, but really think about a scaling recipe.

511
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:29,000
And a scaling recipe is a mapping from a flood budget, let's say 1e25 or 1e24, to a set of hyperparameters, basically a config file.

512
00:46:29,000 --> 00:46:42,000
And for a given scaling recipe, what we will do is run a bunch of experiments to compute the loss that you get at smaller scales.

513
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:48,000
And then you fit a scaling law and that enables you to predict the loss at a target scale.

514
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:56,000
So, maybe you run some small experiments, you fit the scaling law, and then you predict out what you're going to get at larger scale.

515
00:46:56,000 --> 00:46:58,000
Okay. So, that's the primitive.

516
00:46:58,000 --> 00:47:09,000
Now, using this, what you can do is now you can optimize the target, the scaling recipe, targeting at a larger scale using smaller scale experiments, which is wonderful.

517
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:18,000
And second of all, you can predict the loss that you're going to, in theory, achieve before actually running the experiment.

518
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:29,000
Which goes, allows you to go, you know, raise money and you say, well, look, I ran the small scale experiments and I think I can get a really, like a GPT-5 level model.

519
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:32,000
Please give me a lot of money so I can train that model.

520
00:47:32,000 --> 00:47:43,000
So, one thing I think is maybe another misconception is that scaling laws are not laws of nature.

521
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:46,000
They don't just happen automatically.

522
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:49,000
You kind of have to will them into existence.

523
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:54,000
And this happens by careful construction of a scaling recipe, right?

524
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:57,000
And a scaling recipe, remember, it has to extrapolate.

525
00:47:57,000 --> 00:48:10,000
So, what this typically means is that you have a sequence of hyperparameters, which say as the scale increases, maybe the learning rate is a constant, maybe it drops, maybe the batch size increases, but how much?

526
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:14,000
And these are things that a scaling recipe has to figure out.

527
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:30,000
And so you parameterize, so then, in order to get these predictable scaling laws, one thing that you actually have to think about is, how do you parameterize the model in a way to get what's called hyperparameter transfer?

528
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:37,000
Right, meaning that these hyperparameters you use at small scale are either the ones that you use at larger scale or are predictable functions of it.

529
00:48:37,000 --> 00:48:50,000
Right, because at every scale, your learning rate is like sometimes 1e-5 and sometimes 1e-4, then you're not going to be able to magically guess the right learning rate when you are at larger scale.

530
00:48:50,000 --> 00:48:56,000
So, one shift in thinking is that the predictability is actually at least as important as optimality.

531
00:48:56,000 --> 00:49:05,000
So, you normally think, oh, we're trying to optimize for efficiency here and we want to hyperparameter tune and make things optimal.

532
00:49:05,000 --> 00:49:12,000
And, yes, you do want to do that, but you also want this predictability so that you don't get surprised at larger scale.

533
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:16,000
Okay, so that's, this is kind of a stage setting.

534
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:22,000
The actual scaling laws we're going to look at are fairly, you know, classic.

535
00:49:22,000 --> 00:49:31,000
So these are, you know, some of you might have seen this idea of, well, if I give you a flops budget, should you train,

536
00:49:31,000 --> 00:49:35,000
how should you balance training a larger model versus training on more tokens?

537
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:39,000
And this is where the classic compute optimal scaling laws from Kaplan et al.

538
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:42,000
And the chinchilla, so-called chinchilla scaling laws comes in.

539
00:49:42,000 --> 00:50:03,000
The basic idea is that you, for each flops budget, so let's say 6E18 all the way to 3E21, you sweep across different, you know, model sizes.

540
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:08,000
And you choose the best one, so think about minimizing each of these.

541
00:50:08,000 --> 00:50:16,000
And then you fit a curve that allows you to basically predict the number of parameters given a flops budget.

542
00:50:16,000 --> 00:50:20,000
And if you're lucky, it will lie on a, roughly on a line.

543
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:27,000
And if you're unlucky, it's going to be all over the place, which means that you should have no confidence that you're going to be able to predict reliably.

544
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:39,000
And so, you know, the upshot of this is, this is quite crude, but, you know, a rule of thumb is,

545
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:45,000
the 20 times the number of parameters is the number of data points you should train on.

546
00:50:45,000 --> 00:50:50,000
So, this 1B parameter model should be trained on roughly 1.4 trillion tokens.

547
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:56,000
Of course, depending on the data set and architecture, this number will actually vary.

548
00:50:56,000 --> 00:51:01,000
Okay, also this doesn't take into account the inference costs.

549
00:51:01,000 --> 00:51:06,000
A lot of models these days are small, but they're trained on way more tokens than is compute optimal,

550
00:51:06,000 --> 00:51:09,000
because you want a smaller model for inference reasons.

551
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:16,000
Okay, so one fun thing that we've been doing in the Marin project is pre-registering our results.

552
00:51:16,000 --> 00:51:22,000
So we fit a bunch of scaling, you know, plots at different compute budgets.

553
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:30,000
We fit a scaling law, and we basically made these predictions out to 1E22 flops.

554
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:31,000
This one is actually training.

555
00:51:31,000 --> 00:51:34,000
If you go to the Marin site, you can follow along.

556
00:51:34,000 --> 00:51:37,000
It should actually be done maybe as early as tonight.

557
00:51:37,000 --> 00:51:46,000
So maybe on Wednesday I'll report back on how we did and see how we match the pre-registered loss.

558
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:51,000
So the idea here is that we made a prediction on if we were to train this large model,

559
00:51:51,000 --> 00:51:55,000
which we've never trained before, if we can predict how well it's going to do,

560
00:51:55,000 --> 00:51:59,000
then that's really nice.

561
00:51:59,000 --> 00:52:06,000
Okay, so in assignment three, I think this is a kind of either fun or, you know,

562
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:08,000
actually it's a fun assignment.

563
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:10,000
Let's just say it's a fun assignment.

564
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:18,000
So we're going to define this training API, which basically you give us hyperparameters,

565
00:52:18,000 --> 00:52:20,000
and we're going to give you a loss back.

566
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:25,000
So what we're going to try to do is simulate what happens if you could do a lot of

567
00:52:25,000 --> 00:52:26,000
training runs.

568
00:52:26,000 --> 00:52:30,000
And of course we don't have enough compute to actually allow everyone to train their

569
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:33,000
own, you know, 8B model or anything.

570
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:39,000
So basically we train a bunch of models offline and we basically provide this cache

571
00:52:39,000 --> 00:52:41,000
so it looks like you're training.

572
00:52:41,000 --> 00:52:44,000
And what you're going to do is submit training jobs.

573
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:49,000
You give us a config, basically we give you a loss back.

574
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:51,000
And then you can do whatever you want.

575
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:57,000
We would recommend you fit scaling laws to these points, and then you extrapolate,

576
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:04,000
and then we give you a budget and you're, we basically evaluate how well your model landed.

577
00:53:04,000 --> 00:53:09,000
So it is meant to, I was going to say, it's meant to kind of replicate the high stress

578
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:16,000
scenarios if you actually had a budget that, like, you know, $100 million that you did to spend.

579
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:20,000
And you have to be very careful about what, how you spend this.

580
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:27,000
Of course this is low stakes.

581
00:53:27,000 --> 00:53:33,000
Okay. So at this point you will have, you train a model,

582
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:36,000
you know how to make it fast, you know how to scale up.

583
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:39,000
You know, now what's missing?

584
00:53:39,000 --> 00:53:42,000
You know, what do you train the model on?

585
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:48,000
And that's going to be the subject of the data section, which is, you know,

586
00:53:48,000 --> 00:53:54,000
arguably one of the most important things because data quality is basically

587
00:53:54,000 --> 00:53:57,000
specifies how good your model is going to be.

588
00:53:57,000 --> 00:54:02,000
One way to also frame it is, you know, what do you want your model to do?

589
00:54:02,000 --> 00:54:07,000
Right? Data basically is, reflects what your model wants.

590
00:54:07,000 --> 00:54:10,000
So do you want to speak multiple languages?

591
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:13,000
Be good at, you know, having a conversation?

592
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:17,000
Do you want to do, run long agentic coding tasks?

593
00:54:17,000 --> 00:54:22,000
And so part of that is also going to, you know, so we're going to start by

594
00:54:22,000 --> 00:54:27,000
talking about evaluation, which basically defines the capabilities that you would

595
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:29,000
like your model to have.

596
00:54:29,000 --> 00:54:35,000
You know, one thing we'll talk about is, evaluation is a fairly, you know,

597
00:54:35,000 --> 00:54:37,000
deep topic.

598
00:54:37,000 --> 00:54:41,000
Not just, it's not just about like, you know, running on some benchmarks.

599
00:54:41,000 --> 00:54:46,000
There are internal evaluation metrics for model development.

600
00:54:46,000 --> 00:54:52,000
And what matters here is that, you know, smoothness across scales so that there,

601
00:54:52,000 --> 00:54:55,000
You know, remember we want things to be predictable.

602
00:54:55,000 --> 00:54:57,000
Relative performance matters.

603
00:54:57,000 --> 00:55:03,000
You don't care necessarily how well this, you know, does in absolute terms because,

604
00:55:03,000 --> 00:55:09,000
you know, let's say a perplexity number is just like, what does a perplexity of 1.2 on some

605
00:55:09,000 --> 00:55:12,000
you know, held-out data really mean?

606
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:14,000
And then there's external metrics.

607
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:21,000
These are things that you report to your customers or your reviewers or whoever you're

608
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:23,000
presenting your thing to.

609
00:55:23,000 --> 00:55:26,000
And here, ecological validity really matters.

610
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:31,000
And I think sometimes these two things get conflated, but I think they really serve two distinct purposes.

611
00:55:31,000 --> 00:55:38,000
And so you can think about perplexity as something that is really helpful for internal development.

612
00:55:38,000 --> 00:55:47,000
And still to this day, you know, perplexity is a very good way of capturing the intrinsic quality of a model

613
00:55:47,000 --> 00:55:51,000
without getting, kind of worrying about benchmark-maxing.

614
00:55:51,000 --> 00:55:56,000
Now, there's a separate question of what you run your evals on.

615
00:55:56,000 --> 00:56:01,000
And the recommended thing is, well, if you have some data that's not on the Internet,

616
00:56:01,000 --> 00:56:05,000
that would be good because you can avoid contamination.

617
00:56:05,000 --> 00:56:16,000
Okay, so, and then there's advanced use cases which are more representative of external-facing use cases.

618
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:23,000
So, one thing to also note is that, you know, language models are purportedly very general-purpose.

619
00:56:23,000 --> 00:56:30,000
Right? So, it's suitable, it's only fitting that we actually need a very diverse set of evaluations.

620
00:56:30,000 --> 00:56:36,000
And I would always recommend having many evaluations that look at, you can average them into a single number,

621
00:56:36,000 --> 00:56:39,000
but often that average conflates a lot of different things.

622
00:56:39,000 --> 00:56:45,000
Okay, so now after we set up the evals, we know what we're building.

