How do you get the data?

624
00:56:48,000 --> 00:56:53,000
Well, first thing is that data does not just fall from the sky.

625
00:56:53,000 --> 00:56:56,000
It has to be actively, you know, curated.

626
00:56:56,000 --> 00:57:02,000
Often, I think, you know, especially in classes and also in research,

627
00:57:02,000 --> 00:57:07,000
sometimes you're just given a data set and then it's like, okay, well, now I do stuff on the data set.

628
00:57:07,000 --> 00:57:11,000
But a lot of language models, especially if you want to collect these large data sets,

629
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:14,000
you have to go and actively look at it.

630
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:16,000
So, web pages are crawled from the Internet.

631
00:57:16,000 --> 00:57:20,000
There's books, I guess, which is controversial at this point, but

632
00:57:20,000 --> 00:57:23,000
archive papers, GitHub code, and so on.

633
00:57:23,000 --> 00:57:27,000
This is an old figure from the Pile from 2021.

634
00:57:27,000 --> 00:57:32,000
And you can see, you know, language modeling data sets are fairly diverse.

635
00:57:32,000 --> 00:57:41,000
There's, especially these days, I think, a lot of contention around, is it fair use to train on copyrighted data?

636
00:57:41,000 --> 00:57:45,000
Maybe sometimes you have to license data and so on.

637
00:57:45,000 --> 00:57:50,000
So, there's legal issues around data, which I think are quite, you know, important.

638
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:53,000
For example, a lot of GitHub code doesn't have a license.

639
00:57:53,000 --> 00:57:55,000
So, how do you interpret that?

640
00:57:55,000 --> 00:58:00,000
Do you think, assume it's permissive or do you be conservative and assume it's not permissive?

641
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:09,000
So, there's also the fact that data is not even text, right?

642
00:58:09,000 --> 00:58:12,000
It's either HTML or PDFs or code or directories.

643
00:58:12,000 --> 00:58:18,000
And this requires processing to turn it into actual text to be usable for training.

644
00:58:18,000 --> 00:58:21,000
So, that's the topic of data processing.

645
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:24,000
There are a few steps that have to happen here.

646
00:58:24,000 --> 00:58:28,000
Transformation, converting some non-text into text.

647
00:58:28,000 --> 00:58:33,000
Filtering, keeping only the good stuff.

648
00:58:33,000 --> 00:58:43,000
If the random internet document in Common Crawl is extremely bad and you don't want to train on it, most likely.

649
00:58:43,000 --> 00:58:48,000
You want to deduplicate. There's multiple sources.

650
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:51,000
So, how do you combine the different sources?

651
00:58:51,000 --> 00:58:58,000
And then finally, more recently, there's been a lot of work on generating synthetic data,

652
00:58:58,000 --> 00:59:04,000
which could mean taking the real data and just rewriting it into things that are more like the downstream task

653
00:59:04,000 --> 00:59:09,000
or just more Wikipedia-like or whatever you want.

654
00:59:09,000 --> 00:59:13,000
So, this is an active area of research.

655
00:59:13,000 --> 00:59:18,000
Also, data can be used both for pre-training, something called mid-training,

656
00:59:18,000 --> 00:59:25,000
which is usually the high-quality data that you put at the end of the pre-training step.

657
00:59:25,000 --> 00:59:31,000
And this includes long context data such as maybe big code repositories or books.

658
00:59:31,000 --> 00:59:37,000
And then finally, there's post-training data, which are maybe conversations or

659
00:59:37,000 --> 00:59:40,000
agentic traces with tool calling.

660
00:59:40,000 --> 00:59:49,000
So, in assignment four, we're going to make you start with a very raw corpus,

661
00:59:49,000 --> 01:00:00,000
like a raw web crawl, and do all the work to filter and to dedupe and make the data clean.

662
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:08,000
So, this is, I would say, I don't know if I would call it not fun, but it is certainly, I think,

663
01:00:08,000 --> 01:00:13,000
a lot of, you know, what people would call, you know, dirty work.

664
01:00:13,000 --> 01:00:17,000
But that is, I think, part of, an important part of building a language model from scratch.

665
01:00:17,000 --> 01:00:19,000
So, you have to get the full experience.

666
01:00:19,000 --> 01:00:25,000
Okay. So, finally, alignment.

667
01:00:25,000 --> 01:00:30,000
So far, we've basically trained a model using full supervision.

668
01:00:30,000 --> 01:00:32,000
Predict the next token or the next few tokens.

669
01:00:32,000 --> 01:00:37,000
Now, at this point, the model should already be reasonable.

670
01:00:37,000 --> 01:00:43,000
But we can improve it further by using weak supervision.

671
01:00:43,000 --> 01:00:50,000
And why use weak supervision? It's because sometimes it's easier to critique than it is to generate.

672
01:00:50,000 --> 01:00:54,000
So, you can't always have data that says, this is the right response to this prompt,

673
01:00:54,000 --> 01:00:58,000
but maybe you can have a way of specifying what good looks like.

674
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:02,000
So, then, the basic template is that you generate responses from a model.

675
01:01:02,000 --> 01:01:09,000
You score them either with a human or verifier or LLM judge, and then you update the model to prefer better responses.

676
01:01:09,000 --> 01:01:17,000
This can be instantiated either through various RL algorithms such as PPO or GRPO, or in a simpler,

677
01:01:17,000 --> 01:01:22,000
if for preference data, DPO.

678
01:01:22,000 --> 01:01:32,000
So, the challenges around RL are that RL algorithms are unstable and hard to tune.

679
01:01:32,000 --> 01:01:36,000
Some of you probably know this from first-hand experience.

680
01:01:36,000 --> 01:01:40,000
Personally, I prefer to keep things as much in the full,

681
01:01:40,000 --> 01:01:44,000
supervised case as long as possible, and then finally, okay, fine, I have to do RL.

682
01:01:44,000 --> 01:01:49,000
But, you know, some people, for whatever reason, like doing RL.

683
01:01:49,000 --> 01:01:58,000
Also, what we'll hopefully talk about this year is that if you do RL at scale and try to maximize your throughput,

684
01:01:58,000 --> 01:02:01,000
there's actually a lot of systems challenges.

685
01:02:01,000 --> 01:02:07,000
You have to have an inference server and a training server, and then the inference server has to generate these rollouts,

686
01:02:07,000 --> 01:02:11,000
especially if you do RL against environments that involve code execution.

687
01:02:11,000 --> 01:02:13,000
It's a whole kind of orchestration game.

688
01:02:13,000 --> 01:02:28,000
And then, if your workers lag behind, or then you get into off-policy issues, and then you're constantly kind of juggling this on-policy-ness with a desire to kind of maximize throughput.

689
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:33,000
So, to put it, it's a big, wonderful mess, which hopefully we'll talk more about when we come to that.

690
01:02:33,000 --> 01:02:36,000
So, assignment five.

691
01:02:36,000 --> 01:02:40,000
We're still deciding what exactly we want to do.

692
01:02:40,000 --> 01:02:46,000
Last year it was implement DPO and GRPO and get it working for some, you know, math benchmark.

693
01:02:46,000 --> 01:02:53,000
But, we'll see how much farther we can push it on the realistic dimension this year.

694
01:02:53,000 --> 01:02:58,000
Okay. So, again, remember it's about efficiency.

695
01:02:58,000 --> 01:03:03,000
And efficiency can be either data efficiency or compute efficiency.

696
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:06,000
So, the way to think about it, you have all these resources.

697
01:03:06,000 --> 01:03:12,000
You have data, you have the hardware, which has compute cores, you have memory, communication bandwidth.

698
01:03:12,000 --> 01:03:20,000
And you're just trying to figure out how do you build the best model according to some evaluation given a fixed set of resources.

699
01:03:20,000 --> 01:03:29,000
So, you can, through this lens, I think you can actually think about a lot of these design decisions as optimizing for this.

700
01:03:29,000 --> 01:03:33,000
So, systems, clearly that's about compute efficiency.

701
01:03:33,000 --> 01:03:42,000
Tokenization, as I mentioned before, it's, you know, you can just work with raw bytes, but that's going to be very compute inefficient.

702
01:03:42,000 --> 01:03:46,000
And, at least with today's model architectures.

703
01:03:46,000 --> 01:03:50,000
And so, a lot of tokenization is about improving compute efficiency.

704
01:03:50,000 --> 01:03:58,000
Model architecture, many of the changes that we'll see are motivated by reducing the memory or flops.

705
01:03:58,000 --> 01:04:03,000
In fact, a lot of them are influenced by the need to have faster inference.

706
01:04:03,000 --> 01:04:13,000
Data filtering, you can also view as, through efficiency lens, we don't want to waste time updating gradients on a bunch of redundant bad data.

707
01:04:13,000 --> 01:04:20,000
Even if it might not hurt you, but it hurts you in the sense that if you have a fixed compute budget, more time on bad data means less time on good data.

708
01:04:20,000 --> 01:04:30,000
And then finally, scaling laws is explicitly about how you can essentially do effective hyperparameter tuning on much smaller models.

709
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:38,000
And now, tomorrow we might become data constrained, and the calculus of what design decisions you should take might change.

710
01:04:38,000 --> 01:04:45,000
But I think the overall mindset, which we're trying to teach you, is, you know, think about the efficiency of your approach.

711
01:04:45,000 --> 01:04:49,000
Okay. Let me stop there.

712
01:04:49,000 --> 01:05:06,000
Are there any questions about any of these assignments or topics?

713
01:05:06,000 --> 01:05:10,000
Okay. So now let's do tokenization.

714
01:05:10,000 --> 01:05:15,000
So this is, we're jumping into our first unit here.

715
01:05:15,000 --> 01:05:18,000
So Andre Karpathy has this really good video on tokenization.

716
01:05:18,000 --> 01:05:21,000
You should check it out.

717
01:05:21,000 --> 01:05:26,000
So, the starting point is, raw text is, what is text?

718
01:05:26,000 --> 01:05:28,000
It's Unicode strings.

719
01:05:28,000 --> 01:05:36,000
And on the other hand, the language model places the distribution over sequences of tokens, usually represent as indices.

720
01:05:36,000 --> 01:05:45,000
So we need a procedure that encodes these strings into tokens, and also a procedure that decodes tokens back into strings.

721
01:05:45,000 --> 01:05:50,000
So a tokenizer is basically something that can do this round trip.

722
01:05:50,000 --> 01:05:54,000
So here are some examples to give you a flavor for how tokenizers work.

723
01:05:54,000 --> 01:05:58,000
Actually, I should have tried to get internet earlier, so this is not going to work.

724
01:05:58,000 --> 01:05:59,000
Okay.

725
01:05:59,000 --> 01:06:03,000
If you go to the site, you can play around with different tokenizers.

726
01:06:03,000 --> 01:06:08,000
So some observations here.

727
01:06:08,000 --> 01:06:14,000
And you'll appreciate why tokenizers are kind of annoying and why people want to get rid of them.

728
01:06:14,000 --> 01:06:24,000
So a word and its, and a word conglomerated with its preceding space are different, you know, tokens.

729
01:06:24,000 --> 01:06:31,000
So many tokens you'll actually see are space a word, which is, you know, fine but kind of strange.

730
01:06:31,000 --> 01:06:40,000
So this hello and this hello is actually two completely different indices that have nothing to do with each other.

731
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:47,000
So, and sometimes depending on tokenizer you use, numbers are represented with,

732
01:06:47,000 --> 01:06:50,000
every few digits is a token.

733
01:06:50,000 --> 01:06:52,000
Sometimes it's predictable and sometimes it's not.

734
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:57,000
Some tokenizers try to make every digit a token, but then you're blowing up the number of tokens you have.

735
01:06:57,000 --> 01:06:59,000
So there's some trade-off there.

736
01:06:59,000 --> 01:07:04,000
So here's the GPT-5 tokenizer.

737
01:07:04,000 --> 01:07:13,000
So you can take this string and convert it into, you know, these indices.

738
01:07:13,000 --> 01:07:21,000
And then you can decode it back into the string and assume tokenizers should round trip.

739
01:07:21,000 --> 01:07:23,000
If you implement tokenizers, it doesn't round trip, you have a problem.

740
01:07:23,000 --> 01:07:32,000
So the compression ratio here is the number of bytes per token.

741
01:07:32,000 --> 01:07:38,000
So in this case, we have the number of bytes is, of this string is 20.

742
01:07:38,000 --> 01:07:40,000
The number of tokens is 8.

743
01:07:40,000 --> 01:07:43,000
And you do 20 divided by 8, so the compression ratio is 2.5.

744
01:07:43,000 --> 01:07:46,000
Okay, so 2.5 bytes per token.

745
01:07:46,000 --> 01:07:52,000
The larger the compression ratio, that means the shorter the sentence, which is good because attention is quadratic and you want to make sure the sentence is

746
01:07:52,000 --> 01:07:53,000
shorter.

747
01:07:53,000 --> 01:07:59,000
Now you can obviously increase the compression ratio by increasing the vocab size.

748
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:09,000
But then you get into sparsity where more and more, because every element of vocab is treated like a distinct element.

749
01:08:09,000 --> 01:08:21,000
So these days, tokenizers, especially multilingual tokenizers, have 100k or 200k tokens, distinct tokens.

750
01:08:21,000 --> 01:08:25,000
So you can look at the GPT token vocabulary.

751
01:08:25,000 --> 01:08:28,000
I think we'll skip it in the interest of time.

752
01:08:28,000 --> 01:08:30,000
Okay, so how do you build a tokenizer?

753
01:08:30,000 --> 01:08:33,000
So I'm going to go through this fast.

754
01:08:33,000 --> 01:08:40,000
But so the first thing you might do is like, well, Unicode string, that's a sequence already of Unicode characters.

755
01:08:40,000 --> 01:08:46,000
And each character is an integer, which you can call ORD in Python and you get some number out.

756
01:08:46,000 --> 01:08:49,000
And this can be converted into characters.

757
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:55,000
So let's just build a character level tokenizer, which basically breaks up each character and

758
01:08:55,000 --> 01:08:59,000
encodes it in a token. And then this can decode back.

759
01:08:59,000 --> 01:09:04,000
Life is good. So now, there are 150k Unicode characters.

760
01:09:04,000 --> 01:09:12,000
So your vocab size could be 150k, which is, which is, you know, a lot.

761
01:09:12,000 --> 01:09:13,000
I mean, it's not crazy.

762
01:09:13,000 --> 01:09:19,000
But I think the bigger problem is that many characters are actually rare, which means that

763
01:09:19,000 --> 01:09:23,000
it's really an inefficient use of a vocabulary.

764
01:09:23,000 --> 01:09:29,000
And also the compression ratio, which kind of reflects this, is not that great.

765
01:09:29,000 --> 01:09:35,000
So most of the time you're actually using a lot of tokens to represent your sequence.

766
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:41,000
And many of the indices are actually not being very used.

767
01:09:41,000 --> 01:09:44,000
So this is like not a very good tokenizer.

768
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:47,000
So here's another attempt.

769
01:09:47,000 --> 01:09:53,000
So you can turn strings into bytes. So Unicode has a UTF-8

770
01:09:53,000 --> 01:10:02,000
Unicode encoding, which means that you can sometimes, a string like A is just one byte.

771
01:10:02,000 --> 01:10:06,000
And sometimes a string is, you know, multiple bytes.

772
01:10:06,000 --> 01:10:09,000
So let's build a tokenizer around that.

773
01:10:09,000 --> 01:10:15,000
So we can take this string and convert it into a sequence of bytes.

774
01:10:15,000 --> 01:10:18,000
And notice that this is a longer sequence now.

775
01:10:18,000 --> 01:10:27,000
But all the numbers are between 0 and 255 because that's what a byte means.

776
01:10:27,000 --> 01:10:34,000
And the compression ratio is one, which is not great.

777
01:10:34,000 --> 01:10:38,000
Right? So byte sequences can be very long.

778
01:10:38,000 --> 01:10:42,000
But the vocab size is small.

779
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:47,000
Okay, so now, okay, so these both of these are like really bad.

780
01:10:47,000 --> 01:10:50,000
So here's, let's try to make some progress.

781
01:10:50,000 --> 01:10:55,000
So this is what actually people used to do in NLP, if people remember.

782
01:10:55,000 --> 01:10:59,000
So if you take a string, I can just chunk it up into,

783
01:10:59,000 --> 01:11:02,000
break it up by spaces or some regular expression.

784
01:11:02,000 --> 01:11:09,000
And let's just call each of these chunks a token.

785
01:11:09,000 --> 01:11:19,000
Okay, so what is good about this one is that each token is meaningful because humans invented words and words tend to have a stable semantic meaning.

786
01:11:19,000 --> 01:11:25,000
So then, but your vocab size is a number of distinct chunks in the training data, which could be a lot.

787
01:11:25,000 --> 01:11:33,000
And also your compression ratio, I mean, is quite good, but the vocabulary can be huge.

788
01:11:33,000 --> 01:11:40,000
Actually, it's worse than that because the vocabulary could be actually unbounded.

789
01:11:40,000 --> 01:11:41,000
Right?

790
01:11:41,000 --> 01:11:47,000
Because at test time you might get some sequence and you tokenize and then you have a token you've never seen before.

791
01:11:47,000 --> 01:11:55,000
And people used to assign these like unk token, but that's like really ugly and can mess up your perplexity calculations.

792
01:11:55,000 --> 01:11:58,000
So this is also not great.

793
01:11:58,000 --> 01:12:03,000
Okay, so what we're actually going to do is called byte-pair encoding.

794
01:12:03,000 --> 01:12:12,000
And this was introduced a long time ago for data compression, way before language models were really on the scene, really.

795
01:12:12,000 --> 01:12:25,000
It was first introduced to NLP for doing neural machine translation, and the first paper that used BPE for

796
01:12:25,000 --> 01:12:27,000
LMs was GPT2.

797
01:12:27,000 --> 01:12:35,000
So the basic idea is you're going to train the tokenizer on raw text to construct a vocabulary that's tailored to the data.

798
01:12:35,000 --> 01:12:45,000
And you're also going to have this property that everything becomes, can be tokenized if it's rare, then it just breaks up into smaller units rather than having this unk token.

799
01:12:45,000 --> 01:12:52,000
So common sequences are going to be represented as one token.

800
01:12:52,000 --> 01:12:54,000
Rare sequences are going to be split into multiple tokens.

801
01:12:54,000 --> 01:12:55,000
That's the idea.

802
01:12:55,000 --> 01:12:59,000
Okay, so the algorithm is fairly simple conceptually.

803
01:12:59,000 --> 01:13:03,000
So you start basically with your corpus.

804
01:13:03,000 --> 01:13:05,000
Let's assume it's one long sequence.

805
01:13:05,000 --> 01:13:08,000
You get a byte sequence. Each byte starts as a token.

806
01:13:08,000 --> 01:13:16,000
And then we're going to merge successive pairs of adjacent tokens that occur the most frequently.

807
01:13:16,000 --> 01:13:20,000
Okay, so let's step through how this is going to work in code.

808
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:23,000
So here's a simple string, the cat and the hat.

809
01:13:23,000 --> 01:13:27,000
And here's the implementation of the BPE algorithm.

810
01:13:27,000 --> 01:13:32,000
So we're going to turn that into a sequence of bytes.

811
01:13:32,000 --> 01:13:42,000
And then we're going to first basically count the number of times successive tokens appear.

812
01:13:42,000 --> 01:13:48,000
So 116, 104 shows up twice. So we get this.

813
01:13:48,000 --> 01:13:56,000
And then we're going to find the pair that happens the most number of times.

814
01:13:56,000 --> 01:13:58,000
So that's 116, 104.

815
01:13:58,000 --> 01:14:01,000
I guess there's a few ties, but we'll just take the first one.

816
01:14:01,000 --> 01:14:03,000
And then we're going to merge that pair.

817
01:14:03,000 --> 01:14:08,000
And by merging that pair, what we do is we create a new token.

818
01:14:08,000 --> 01:14:13,000
In this case, this is going to be called token 256.

819
01:14:13,000 --> 01:14:16,000
It's going to represent this pair.

820
01:14:16,000 --> 01:14:19,000
And we're going to add it to our vocabulary.

821
01:14:19,000 --> 01:14:23,000
So 256 is going to represent the sequence th.

822
01:14:23,000 --> 01:14:25,000
So t and h have been merged.

823
01:14:25,000 --> 01:14:30,000
And we're going to call every time we see th, that's going to, we're going to use 256 to represent that.

824
01:14:30,000 --> 01:14:37,000
And then we go through indices and then we replace every occurrence of 116, 104 with 256.

825
01:14:37,000 --> 01:14:41,000
So those two places have been replaced. And then we iterate.

826
01:14:41,000 --> 01:14:48,000
So the next time we do this, we're going to find 256 and 101.

827
01:14:48,000 --> 01:14:52,000
We're going to merge that. And now we have 257.

828
01:14:52,000 --> 01:14:58,000
And then we're going to merge that one more time and we're going to get 258.

829
01:14:58,000 --> 01:15:05,000
So over time, the sequence is shrinking, and the vocabulary size is growing.

830
01:15:05,000 --> 01:15:07,000
Okay.

831
01:15:07,000 --> 01:15:20,000
So let me, and the compression ratio here is, that we get for this, I mean, this toy example is 1.5.

832
01:15:20,000 --> 01:15:27,000
Okay, so now that you have a tokenizer, how do you tokenize new text?

833
01:15:27,000 --> 01:15:31,000
Well, you take a new string and you encode it.

834
01:15:31,000 --> 01:15:39,000
And conceptually what happens is that you basically go through the set of merges that you've made.

835
01:15:39,000 --> 01:15:44,000
And then you just apply the merges to your string.

836
01:15:44,000 --> 01:15:47,000
Okay. Let me actually not step through that code.

837
01:15:47,000 --> 01:15:51,000
So that will give you a sequence.

838
01:15:51,000 --> 01:15:56,000
So this is a sequence encoding of the Quick Brown Fox.

839
01:15:56,000 --> 01:16:00,000
And then when you decode it, you get the same thing back.

840
01:16:00,000 --> 01:16:05,000
So, I went through this a bit fast, just in the interest of time.

841
01:16:05,000 --> 01:16:11,000
I will say that this implementation works.

842
01:16:11,000 --> 01:16:15,000
This is a full-blown PP implementation. It's extremely slow.

843
01:16:15,000 --> 01:16:22,000
So in assignment one, we're going to ask you to basically make it faster.

844
01:16:22,000 --> 01:16:28,000
So currently encode loops over all the merges,

845
01:16:28,000 --> 01:16:32,000
which is very slow because the number of merges you have is essentially,

846
01:16:32,000 --> 01:16:36,000
essentially, the vocab size minus 256.

847
01:16:36,000 --> 01:16:40,000
So you only want to loop over the merges that matter.

848
01:16:40,000 --> 01:16:44,000
And you have to build some indices to make that happen.

849
01:16:44,000 --> 01:16:48,000
There's some details around special tokens.

850
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:51,000
Conceptually not deep, but important to building a modern tokenizer.

851
01:16:51,000 --> 01:16:56,000
Another thing is that I've presented the tokenizer just for simplicity,

852
01:16:56,000 --> 01:16:59,000
as you take an entire string and then you try to tokenize it.

853
01:16:59,000 --> 01:17:08,000
Really what happens is that you break up your text into chunks and then you apply tokenizer on each chunk.

854
01:17:08,000 --> 01:17:10,000
So that's going to be much faster.

855
01:17:10,000 --> 01:17:15,000
And then try to make it as fast as possible.

856
01:17:15,000 --> 01:17:21,000
At some point you might realize that Python is just not very fast and if you want to implement it in,

857
01:17:21,000 --> 01:17:28,000
some of your favorite language, Rust or C or something, then go for it.

858
01:17:28,000 --> 01:17:31,000
Okay, so quick summary.

859
01:17:31,000 --> 01:17:36,000
Tokenizers convert between strings and tokens or indices.

860
01:17:36,000 --> 01:17:44,000
The previous character base, byte base, word base are highly suboptimal in their own way.

861
01:17:44,000 --> 01:17:53,000
So BPE is an effective heuristic that is data driven so it seems to be pretty effective.

862
01:17:53,000 --> 01:18:00,000
Now, like I said before, maybe next year I don't have to teach this but for this year we're stuck with tokenization.

863
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:11,000
Even if we get rid of tokenization though, I think whatever solution replaces it, I think has to satisfy the following properties.

864
01:18:11,000 --> 01:18:18,000
Right? If you have the model, the Transformer needs to operate on some sort of abstractions of the sequence.

865
01:18:18,000 --> 01:18:26,000
And this is most evident if you think about not just text but video or DNA sequences where,

866
01:18:26,000 --> 01:18:34,000
the individual bytes or units are actually quite kind of low signal to noise.

867
01:18:34,000 --> 01:18:40,000
And you have to do some sort of abstraction to lift it into a place where you can do modeling on that.

868
01:18:40,000 --> 01:18:45,000
And then finally, as I mentioned, chunks should be kind of variable.

869
01:18:45,000 --> 01:18:47,000
You want adaptive computation.

870
01:18:47,000 --> 01:18:50,000
Not all bytes are created the same.

871
01:18:50,000 --> 01:18:53,000
And if you don't do that, I think you're going to be suboptimal.

872
01:18:53,000 --> 01:18:57,000
So if any end-to-end solution also, I think, has to have these properties.

